## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to decompose a 128‑bit value into four 32‑bit lanes and re‑assemble them without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Lane extraction via masks and shifts
- 128‑bit as hi/lo pairs with 32‑bit lanes
- Branchless permutation and safe packing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 permute32x4_128(uint128 x) {
  uint32 A = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32 B = (uint32)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint32 C = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32 D = (uint32)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint128 y;
  y.hi = ((uint64_t)B << 32) | A;
  y.lo = ((uint64_t)D << 32) | C;
  return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to permute eight 16‑bit lanes within 128‑bit? 
- What tests validate boundary conditions (e.g., when A/B/C/D are all zeros or all ones)?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

